## Why This Is Asked

Tests critical authorization paths, data-loss prevention, and audit integrity in multi-tenant setups, plus resilience to flaky services and retention policies.

## Key Concepts

- RBAC and DLP enforcement across tenants
- Tamper-evident audit logs (sequencing, signatures/HMAC)
- End-to-end data export redaction
- Seeds and mocks for deterministic, isolated tests
- Flaky service handling and time-based retention checks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo end-to-end test sketch (Playwright/Cypress-agnostic)
async function testRBACAndDLP() {
  const t = seedTenant('tenantA');
  const uOwner = seedUser(t, 'owner');
  const uEditor = seedUser(t, 'editor');
  const uAuditor = seedUser(t, 'auditor');
  const admin = seedUser(t, 'admin');

  // Owner creates a share with PII
  await login(uOwner);
  await createShare({ tenant: t.id, data: 'PII' });

  // Editor attempts restricted action
  await login(uEditor);
  assertAccessDenied(editShare(t.id));

  // Auditor reads audit trail
  await login(uAuditor);
  assertAuditIncludes(t.id, 'READ', uAuditor.id);

  // Admin exports with redaction
  await login(admin);
  const exportPayload = await exportData(t.id);
  assertRedacts(exportPayload, ['PII']);
  // Verify tamper-evident log
  assertTamperEvidence(t.id);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests data isolation, asynchronous ETL, eventual consistency, and idempotency in a real-world multi-tenant dashboard.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- Mocking external data sources
- Idempotent operations
- Eventual consistency and latency
- Regional replication and failover

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright-like sketch
test('dataset ETL end-to-end with tenant isolation', async ({page}) => {
  const tenant = await createTenant();
  const dsId = `ds-${Date.now()}`;
  await loginAsTenantUser(tenant);
  await page.goto('/datasets');
  await page.fill('#name', dsId);
  await page.click('#create');
  // mock external sources and trigger ETL via API
  await mockExternalDataForTenant(tenant, dsId);
  await triggerETL(dsId);
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
  // idempotency check
  await page.click('#create', {force:true});
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
});
```

